Marvel movies aren't always totally  accurate to the comics they adapt and  lots can change between the page and the  big screen unfortunately this means that  some characters end up having some of  their best powers removed here are the  Marvel movie characters with powers you  didn't know about while he's a  formidable hero in the MCU Falcon  actually doesn't have any bonafide  superpowers to speak of in the comics  however Sam Wilson has a much greater  range of abilities Captain America Civil  War introduces Red Wing a flying drone  that Falcon uses against crossbones  terrorists and later in the fight  against the pro Accords heroes in the  comics however Red Wing is an actual  Hawk with whom Sam Wilson shares a  telepathic bond without saying a word  Sam can communicate with Red Wing give  him commands and see through his eyes  not only does Wilson have powers but for  a time the Falcon believes he's actually  a mutant too  the reveal comes in the 1983 through  1984 Falcon miniseries when Wilson  becomes a target of the mutant hunting  Sentinel robots this was revised however  before Falcon could join NEX teams in  2003 Avengers number 64 it's revealed  that while Falcon isn't a mutant he's  still more powerful than ever  initially believing his telepathic bond  only worked between himself and Red Wing  Falcon learns his abilities can work on  any bird as well as whole flocks of  birds simultaneously both the comics and  the film's depict an Incredible Hulk  who's tough to kill but in 2018 the job  of hoaxes enemies got that much tougher  in Avengers number 684 the Hulk is  brought back from the dead having been  put in the ground two years previous by  Hawkeye in Civil War 2 number 3 as part  of the story of his resurrection it's  revealed the Hulk can't be killed at  least not for good reaching far back  into comics of the past the writers  showed multiple times that the Hulk died  only to soon return often with little or  no explanation the story then leads  directly into the series immortal Hulk  in which both Bruce Banner and his alter  ego are repeatedly killed often more  than once in the same issue only to  return within a few hours ironically a  little bit of this has been reflected in  the films but it was actually before the  Hulk's immortal status was established  in the comics in the final battle of  Seventeen's thor ragnarok banner leaps  from the grandmaster spaceship onto the  Bifrost  apparently expecting to transform into  the Hulk before he lands hey it happened  before  instead banner hits the Rainbow Bridge  head-on and it looks like he might  genuinely be dead until moments later  when the Hulk emerges Eddie Brock and  his alien symbiote showed no shortage of  strength in 2018 s venom but in the  comics his list of powers is even longer  for one venom has the ability to mimic  the super powers of any enhanced humans  he bonds with which is why in the comics  long after the symbiote separated from  Peter Parker venom still uses  spider-man's abilities while bonded with  the symbiote Eddie can make himself  appear to be wearing any clothing he  chooses and can even disguise himself as  other people and while venom shows that  the symbiote can heal its human host  when Flash Thompson takes on the  symbiote in the comics it proves able to  do something even more extraordinary  Thompson lost his legs as the result of  wounds he suffered during the Iroquois  but after Thompson bonds with venom the  symbiote grows legs whenever Thompson  uses its powers giving the veteran a  chance to walk whenever he wishes in  Avengers age of Ultron the MC use  version of The Scarlet Witch uses a  range of powers such as telekinesis and  telepathy which only seem to get  stronger as the franchise progresses but  in the comics scarlet witch's abilities  are even more complex and staggeringly  powerful - there's nothing more  and a miracle  in the source material Wanda uses chaos  magic to warp reality on a smaller scale  she might do something like causing  metal to rust so she can break through a  solid wall or if she was threatened by a  crowd of enemies she could release a  blast of magical energy to take them all  down without ever breaking a sweat but  on a larger scale scarlet witch's powers  can have literally world-changing  consequences on multiple occasions for  example she has brought the dead back to  life in the 2004 event Avengers  disassembled she generates whole armies  of enemies like Ultron robots and Kree  warriors to attack the Avengers in the  following years house of M she changes  the face of the world including making  her fellow mutants the dominant world  power then towards the end of the event  she takes away every mutants powers by  infamously stating no more mutants only  the intervention of a certain sorcerer  supreme allows a handful of mutants to  retain their powers in the Incredible  Hulk Bruce Banner and Betty Ross go to  scientist Samuel Sterns for help in  finding a cure for banners condition  after Banner is captured by the military  Emil Blonsky forces Sterne's to inject  him with banners blood turning him into  the abomination the last time Stern's  appears the abomination knocks him to  the ground and banners blood begins  dripping into an open wound on the  scientists head causing his skin to  start bubbling so what's all that about  well in the comics Samuel Sterns is the  hoax arch-nemesis the leader when he  first appears in the comics he's the  polar opposite of banner rather than a  brainy scientist turned into a  super-strong monster by gamma rays he's  a high school dropout made highly  intelligent by the same radiation as  well as taking on a radically different  appearance the leader enjoys beyond  genius intelligence and a host of  psychic powers  he's been shown to use telekinesis and  can also take over people's minds and  have them do his bidding the only  limitation to his mental control over  others is that it doesn't work on people  affected by gamma radiation such as the  Hulk himself one of the few actors from  The Incredible Hulk to have made a  return later in the MCU is William Hertz  Thaddeus Ross Thunderbolt is one of the  hoax oldest enemies and in the comics he  eventually becomes a little more than  just the Secretary of State in 2008 Hulk  number one a new red skinned Hulk shows  up to  havock and it looks like he's even more  powerful than the original its  eventually revealed that this monster is  the general himself as it turns out Ross  got the powers from a group calling  itself the Intelligencia composed of  brainy Marvel villains like MODOK the  red ghost and the mad thinker Red Hulk  has abilities comparable to that of the  original but with one major difference  he can absorb energy to keep making  himself stronger and stronger this added  advantage makes him capable of beating  the heck out of the original halt  Iron Man and Thor among others after  being defeated by the original Hulk the  red hope becomes something of a hero and  he soon welcomed into the ranks of the  Avengers after the events of the 2012  line wide event Avengers vs. x-men Ross  forms his own version of the  Thunderbolts as a means of fighting  injustice without the legal and moral  constraints of the Avengers which would  be a pretty ironic turn up for the MCU  version of the character in her earliest  comic book appearances Betty Ross is  little more than Bruce banners love  interest and usually takes on the role  of damsel in distress but her role in  the Hulk's life has led to a number of  transformations for Betty some of which  have made her physically powerful enough  to handle the green Goliath one-on-one  in 1973's Incredible Hulk numbers 168  and 169 Betty is brainwashed by the  villain MODOK and transformed into the  harpy a gamma-irradiated flying creature  with claws super-strength and the  ability to fire energy blasts from her  hands while her tenure as the avian  monster is brief she proves powerful  enough to ko the Hulk twice in that time  for a much longer stretch Betty takes on  the role of red she-hulk this goes down  when MODOK and the leader revived her  from death and give her similar powers  to Red Hulk as red she-hulk Betty is  brainwashed and largely portrayed as a  villain eventually however she strikes  out on her own as a hero briefly joins  the defenders and even teams up with the  original Hulk best of both worlds while  Betty's red she-hulk powers are forcibly  stripped away from her in 2014 s Hulk  number 8 in later comics she proves even  more powerful in 2019 immortal Hulk  number 14  Betty is shot in the head and killed by  the mercenary Bushwhacker she returns in  later issues as the monstrous red harpy  with a form similar to that she took on  as the  the only red instead of green and that  definitely didn't happen in the movie  the movie version of beast has a lot in  common with his comic counterpart one  aspect of Hank McCoy that isn't depicted  in the films however is his tendency to  dramatically change his physical  appearance sometimes these changes are  explained by plot and other times  they're explained simply by the  character undergoing a secondary  mutation when he first appears in 1963 s  uncanny x-men number one the Beast  doesn't look particularly different from  other humans later Hank drinks a serum  that makes him more powerful and covers  his body in gray fur after a clash with  the villainous Quasimodo in 1972 s  Amazing Adventures number 14 beasts gray  fur turns blue leading to the classic  bouncing blue beast look most fans are  familiar with he remains in this form  for many years with a brief break during  x-factor when he's temporarily made to  look more human when Beast becomes a  regular in Grant Morrison's  game-changing new x-men premiering in  2001 McCoy is still covered with blue  fur but his features take on a more  feline appearance as a result of that  secondary mutation the change makes  McCoy fear he's losing his grip on  humanity and he actually considers  looking into a cure for his mutant gene  in astonishing x-men in all-new x-men  premiering in the early 2010's beast  undergoes yet another secondary mutation  it frees him from the feline form giving  him a bulkier look less hair on his head  and a face that looks a little like a  bats the MCU Drax definitely isn't a guy  you'd want to mess with  [Music]  this version of the character is mostly  inspired by the 2005 Drax the destroyer  miniseries in which the titular hero is  killed and subsequently resurrected in a  less powerful but more intelligent form  but there is an older version of the  hero and this one is far stronger  unlike the version depicted in the  movies the Drax of the comics is  specifically created to destroy Thanos  by Chronos the mad Titans grandfather in  this iteration Drax could fly and fire  powerful energy blasts out of his hands  while Drax rarely seems quite up to the  task of defeating Thanos he holds his  own against him  which is pretty impressive in of itself  after being resurrected just before 1991  s Infinity Gauntlet event Drax is more  powerful than ever though he's afflicted  by diminished intelligence still he  proves himself the physical equal of  Marvel's most powerful heroes including  the Hulk Thor and the Silver Surfer Drax  also seems to have the built-in capacity  to revive himself after death though he  usually changes significantly after a  resurrection there's never any real  explanation as to why he keeps coming  back to life again and again other than  his quest to kill thanos presumably as  long as Stannis is alive Drax will never  be able to stay dead check out one of  our newest videos right here plus even  more looper videos about your favorite  movies are coming soon subscribe to our  YouTube channel and hit the bell so you  don't miss a single one  [Music]

